Finding: {"title":"Reconciliation confirm always reports 'matched' even when the update touched zero rows","severity":"HIGH","area":"Stripe reconciliation — business-rules correctness","files":["src/server/stripe/school-stripe-service.ts"],"detail":"matchInvoiceToExisting (line ~1845-1871) and matchPaymentToExisting (line ~1873-1898) run `db.update(...).where(...)` with no `.returning()` and no row-count check, then do `return updated !== null;`. Drizzle's neon-serverless update() always resolves to a query result object, never `null` — so this check is always true regardless of whether the WHERE clause (matchId + flightSchoolId + isNull(stripeInvoiceId)) actually matched a row. If the admin's decision.matchId is stale, wrong, or the target invoice/payment already has a Stripe id attached (so the `isNull(...)` guard excludes it), the update silently no-ops but the caller (confirmStudentStripeReconciliation, routes-stripe.ts POST /stripe/customers/:studentId/reconciliation/confirm) still reports status 'matched' and increments result.matched.","impact":"Admin performing manual reconciliation is told a Stripe invoice/payment was linked to a local record when it was not. The record stays unmatched (no stripeInvoiceId/stripePaymentIntentId), so it resurfaces as brand_new/suggested_match in the next preview, or worse, the admin believes reconciliation is complete while the local ledger and Stripe stay out of sync — a silent accounting drift in a billing-adjacent feature.","recommendation":"Check actual affected row count: add `.returning({ id: ... })` and check the returned array length, or inspect the result's rowCount/count property for the exact driver in use, instead of `!== null`.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

